RISC-V pool mappings
===================

Run:
  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/riscv_pool_map_test.sh
  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/riscv_map_size_test.sh
  bash tools/testing/selftests/multikernel/memory_teardown_test.sh

The map fixture executes arch/riscv/mm/multikernel.c with modeled page-table,
allocator, pool-resource and TLB boundaries. It covers Sv39/Sv48/Sv57 folding,
alignment/overflow/range rejection, online RAM exclusion, existing leaves,
neighboring allocations, allocation failure at every level, rollback, missing or
wrong-PFN unmap rejection, empty-PTE-table retirement and reuse. Freeing a table
before a global flush after its parent is cleared fails an assertion. This is
host evidence, not page-table execution or a hot-remove/hot-add runtime result.
The memory fixture also exercises actual re-reservation and baseline cleanup.

Contract
--------

Callers own an allocated, page-aligned range contained in one hot-removed pool
chunk. They keep it allocated through map/use/unmap. The common map mutex and
online-memory lock serialize page-table updates with other pool mappings and
hotplug. Range checks do not replace caller ownership or CPU-stop settlement.

Map allocates all page tables before publishing leaf PTEs. Allocation failure
leaves no new leaf mapping, so callers can return the allocation. Existing PTEs
or huge leaves are rejected and never split or overwritten. Unmap validates the
whole range before changing any entry; errors retain its mappings/allocation.
It clears only requested PTEs, then frees empty PTE tables after a global TLB
flush. Empty upper tables remain reusable. Their memory consumption is bounded
by the pool address space visited. The MK-only PMD leaf cap permits those tables
to remain while RAM is re-added; an empty PTE table cannot remain because it
would obstruct PMD-sized create_pmd_mapping() in init.c.

Generic instance teardown propagates unmap errors before releasing control
memory, region metadata or the instance pool. Destruction retains registry and
kernfs membership on this error; retry is possible after correction. CPU/device
return may already have completed, so this is memory retention, not an atomic
rollback of every resource return. Re-reservation and baseline replacement stop
on refusal. Failed-create/final-reference cleanup retains memory and its record
if retirement fails; an unreachable record can require reboot for recovery.
The x86 architecture unmap still returns success after its existing removal;
an unexpected error at its pool-control wrapper retains the physical allocation.

Remaining runtime gate
----------------------

On the exact refreshed stack, remove pool RAM, map/use two neighboring grants,
unmap one without affecting the other, retire both, return RAM, and repeat.
Inject page-table allocation failure and verify allocator ownership and adjacent
RAM/mappings remain intact. Test Sv39 and Sv48 with real QEMU/OpenSBI execution
and record kernel/config/harness hashes. Kernel links and this fixture do not
satisfy that gate. Lifecycle/doorbell/loader integration is a separate prerequisite.
